<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad V of Granada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1338–<a href="/years/1391">1391</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasrid-dynasty">Nasrid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> was the Nasrid sultan of Granada from 1354 to 1359 and again from 1362 to 1391. Deposed in a palace coup, he regained his throne with help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-of-castile">Peter I of Castile</a>. His long second reign was the high point of Nasrid culture, when the Court of the Lions and other celebrated parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a> were built, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-khatib">Ibn al-Khatib</a> and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-zamrak">Ibn Zamrak</a> at court.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-v-of-granada.jpg" alt="Muhammad V of Granada" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Waleedcop2 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhammad_V_Nasrid_gold_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muhammad, who took the regnal name al-Ghani bi'llah, was the eldest son and heir of Yusuf I by his slave Butayna. When Yusuf I was murdered at prayer in the congregational mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> in 1354, his son was still a minor, and the state was run by the hajib Ridwan, the vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-khatib">Ibn al-Khatib</a> and Yahya ibn Umar ibn Raḥḥu, commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazis</a>. They kept peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, paying tribute and sending troops against its enemies when asked, as against Aragon in 1359.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>A palace coup in August 1359 put his half-brother Isma'il II on the throne. Muhammad fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadix">Guadix</a> and then to the Marinid ruler Abu Salim in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-khatib">Ibn al-Khatib</a>, and there both men met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>. Isma'il II was killed in June 1360 by his cousin, who reigned as Muhammad VI. In 1362 Muhammad V returned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-of-castile">Peter I of Castile</a>, and their forces took Málaga; Muhammad VI surrendered to Peter, who executed him.</p><p>In his second reign Muhammad V steered through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castilian-civil-war">Castilian Civil War</a>, backing Peter against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-castile">Henry of Trastámara</a>, making peace with Henry when he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> in 1366 and renewing his loyalty to Peter in 1367. After Peter was assassinated in 1369 he recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a> and destroyed it. In 1370 he abolished the office of chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazis</a>, placing the Zenata troops under the direct command of the Nasrid family for the first time. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> was relatively free of internal conflict in these years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Besides the Palace of the Lions, he rebuilt the Mexuar and refurbished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comares-palace">Comares Palace</a> with the ornate façade of the Patio del Cuarto Dorado. Between 1365 and 1367 he built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimaristan">Maristan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, one of the earliest European hospitals to care also for the mentally ill. He sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> as an ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> in 1363, and Peter I built his palace in the Alcazar of Seville in the style of Granada. Muhammad V died on 16 January 1391 and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ii">Yusuf II</a>; in the century before Granada fell, the dynasty was torn by succession disputes and assassinations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad V of Granada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.18,-3.6&amp;year=1372" aria-label="Granada on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Muhammad V of Granada"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4755 4384h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4755 4384h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.18,-3.6&amp;year=1372">What was here in 1372? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
